<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sibyl Hathaway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1884">1884</a>–<a href="/years/1974">1974</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sibyl Hathaway was the Dame of Sark, the feudal ruler of the Channel Island of Sark, from 1927 until her death in 1974. During the German occupation of 1940 to 1945 she stayed on the island and dealt firmly with the occupying officers on behalf of its inhabitants. She was made a Dame Commander of the Order of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> in 1965.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sibyl-hathaway.jpg" alt="Sibyl Hathaway" width="320" height="361" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: British government photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sibyl_Hathaway.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sibyl Mary Collings was born in Guernsey, the daughter of William Frederick Collings, seigneur of Sark, and his Montreal-born wife Sophie. As heir presumptive she was raised by her intemperate father as if she were a boy, taught to shoot, sail and climb cliffs despite a limp, and she learned French and the island's Sercquiais dialect. In 1901, after he threw her out of the house at midnight over her love for the painter Dudley Beaumont, she fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and married him at St James's Church, Piccadilly. Beaumont died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-flu">Spanish flu</a> on 24 November 1918, leaving her a pregnant widow with little money; she learned German and worked for the YMCA in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>She succeeded her father on 20 June 1927 and at once reasserted feudal dues such as poulage, a tax of two chickens. To draw tourists she banned motor vehicles and holiday camps. In November 1929 she married Robert Hathaway, an American-born former army aviator, who became seigneur in right of his wife, though she kept the final say.</p><p>During the German occupation from 3 July 1940 to 8 May 1945 she refused to leave and persuaded the native-born islanders to stay. She received German officers with strict etiquette, used her friendship with Eugen Fürst zu Oettingen-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wallerstein">Wallerstein</a>, commander in Guernsey, to get a German army doctor to treat her tenants, and refused to sign German orders. The historian David Fraser notes that she raised no objection to antisemitic orders that affected her Czech Jewish friend Annie Wranowsky. Her eldest son Francis was killed in the Liverpool Blitz, and her husband was deported to an internment camp in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in February 1943. After liberation she was asked to command 275 men of the German garrison and had them clear the island of mines.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1949 she welcomed Princess Elizabeth to open Sark's new harbour, and in 1957 she paid homage to her as queen, the first time the ceremony was performed on the island. Widowed again in 1954, she ruled alone, published her autobiography Dame of Sark in 1961, and in 1969 threatened to surrender the island's charter to the Crown in a dispute with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chief-pleas">Chief Pleas</a> before withdrawing. She died of a heart attack at the Seigneurie on 14 July 1974, aged 90, and was succeeded by her grandson Michael Beaumont. William Douglas-Home's play The Dame of Sark was drawn from her wartime experience.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sibyl Hathaway” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Dame Sibyl Hathaway</b>, Dame of Sark from 1927 to 1974, led the island through German occupation from 1940 to 1945.</blockquote><a href="/state/sark">Sark</a></li></ul></section>
